    subagent_type='explore',
    event=ThinkPart(
        type='think',
        think='This fine ASCII shows NO readable text glyphs — just noisy/blobby patterns. It looks like organic noise, like a blurr
ed or noisy image, not crisp UI text. This looks like... a photograph! The repeated vertical band of "############" at columns ~250-
290 (x≈500-580) from top to bottom looks like an object edge.\n\nHmm wait, maybe this image is actually heavily compressed/blurred, 
or... maybe the PNG contains a photo of a room/screen? The blobs look organic.\n\nHold on — maybe I should reconsider the channel in
terpretation. alphaInfo rawValue 3 = .premultipliedFirst. For kCGBitmapByteOrder32Little + premultipliedFirst, memory order is BGRA.
I used B=ptr[0], G=ptr[1], R=ptr[2] — that\'s what I did in the later scripts (lum uses ptr[off+2] as R). OK.\n\nBut wait — maybe th
e image is NOT premultipliedFirst in the file; `cgImage(forProposedRect:)` could return an image that requires colorsync rendering. 
The blobs might be because we read raw RGB values of a wide-gamut (Display P3-like "LG HDR 4K") image. That shouldn\'t create blobs 
though.\n\nAlternatively — is this image maybe a screenshot with a heavy blur/pixelation filter applied (privacy redaction)? If some
one blurred everything except the menu bar?? The menu bar OCR\'d perfectly ("Safari File Edit View..."). The menu bar region was cri
sp. But window content is blobs.\n\nOR: the screenshot might show the Figma app\'s file browser with file thumbnails, and the blobs 
ARE the thumbnails (design previews = organic shapes), and the TEXT is small and dim (luminance ~40-60 on ~30 background) so my thre
shold render shows text as weak \'+\' scattered. The menu bar text is bright (~167+).\n\nHmm, but per-crop Vision OCR with per-crop 
stretch should\'ve handled dim text... unless the text is tiny. Text height: if this 3840-wide image shows a 1920-pt wide screen at 
2x, UI text 13pt = 26px — plenty. If it\'s 1x (3840-pt screen), text 13px — Vision can still often read 13px text... borderline.\n\n
Menu bar text recognized: "Safari" at fx=0.0276, fw=0.0218 → width 84px, height 0.0129*2160 ≈ 28px. Standard menu bar text at 2x Ret
ina would be 13pt*2=26px tall ✓. So the screen is 1920x1080 points at 2x. So UI text in the window should be ~26px tall and OCR-able
if contrast is OK.\n\nSo why does the window content OCR to nothing and look like blobs at fine scale? Let me look at my fine ASCII 
again: At the top rows there are runs of "####################" — 20-30 chars wide = 40-60px wide blobs. These could be thumbnail ca
rds. The vertical band at x≈500-580 (cols 250-290 in output... wait output chars are 2px each, so col 250 = x 500).\n\nHmm actually 
wait, look at rows 12-30, cols 30-70: "#########################" dense blocks. And there\'s a huge vertical band from cols ~250-300
spanning all rows: that could be a sidebar divider... no, it\'s wide (~100px).\n\nHonestly, this looks like the window content area 
shows large blurred/organic content — like a photo or video playing in Safari, or... Figma design canvas with artwork?\n\nAlternativ
ely the window might be showing Figma canvas with an actual design (photos of products) — the task says "file browser Recents view" 
which has thumbnails of designs (photos!) — blobs = thumbnails. But text labels should still exist.\n\nLet me test the hypothesis di
fferently: check for horizontal text-like line structure. Or simpler: let me OCR with tesseract on a strongly stretched version with
--psm 11. Vision may be failing due to low contrast; tesseract with adaptive thresholding may do better.\n\nLet me generate stretche
d grayscale PNG to stdout and pipe to tesseract with psm 11. Try stretch [19,110].',
        encrypted=None
    )
)
